from factcheck import FactChecker

fc = FactChecker(index_path="index.pkl")

report = fc.check(
    question="I feel a lump on my right breast, what is it and should i get it checked out",
    answer="Yes. Any new breast lump should be evaluated by a healthcare professional, even though many breast lumps are not cancer. A lump can have many possible causes, including: Breast cyst – a fluid-filled sac that can feel smooth or firm and may become tender, especially around your menstrual cycle. Fibroadenoma – a common, noncancerous, firm, rubbery lump that moves easily under the skin, often seen in younger women. Fibrocystic breast changes – benign changes that can cause lumpiness, tenderness, or swelling. Infection or abscess – can cause a painful lump, redness, warmth, and sometimes fever. Fat necrosis – scar-like tissue that can form after an injury to the breast. Breast cancer – while less common than benign causes, a new lump can be a sign of breast cancer and should not be ignored. When you should seek medical evaluation Arrange an appointment with your primary care provider or a breast clinic within the next few days, especially if the lump: Is new and persists after your next menstrual period (if you menstruate). Feels hard or irregular. Does not move easily. Continues to grow. Is associated with skin dimpling, redness, thickening, or nipple changes (such as inversion or bloody discharge). Is accompanied by swollen lymph nodes under your arm. A clinician will likely perform a clinical breast examination and, depending on your age and risk factors, may recommend: An ultrasound (often the first imaging test in younger women). A mammogram (more commonly used in women over 40 or when indicated). A biopsy if imaging suggests the lump needs further evaluation. Seek urgent medical care today if The breast is red, hot, swollen, and you have a fever (possible infection). The lump appeared after a significant injury and the breast is rapidly swelling or becoming very painful. You develop severe symptoms or the skin changes rapidly. While it's understandable to worry, most breast lumps are benign, particularly in younger women. The important thing is to have it assessed rather than trying to determine the cause yourself. If you're comfortable sharing: How old are you? Is the lump painful or painless? Is it hard or soft, and does it move under the skin? When did you first notice it? Have you noticed any nipple discharge or skin changes?",
)
print(report.summary())
print("Total is: "+str(report.support_rate))       # e.g. 0.6 -> 60% of claims supported
print(report.flagged_claims)     # claims MiniCheck couldn't ground